<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swami Vivekananda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Narendranath Datta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Swami Vivekananda was an Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> monk, a disciple of Ramakrishna, who did much to introduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> and yoga to the West. Before his celebrated address to the World's Parliament of Religions in Chicago in 1893 he travelled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a wandering monk, hosted by rulers such as those of Limbdi and Khetri. In 1897 he founded the Ramakrishna Mission, and his teaching shaped modern Hindu reform and Indian nationalism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda.jpg" alt="Swami Vivekananda" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Dziewa at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swami_Vivekananda-1893-09-signed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
